  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{5}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =4\\times \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $16.$",
